ing at specific requirement text, I should have the stitch agent read the SRD directly and map my task requirements to those SRD IDs. That way the full specification is available when the agent implements the solution.\n\nFor acceptance criteria, I'm defining what needs to pass: the build should succeed, linting should be clean, differential tests against the reference implementation should work, and the test suite should pass.\n\nOn design decisions, I want to establish that the implementation should follow existing patterns from the codebase and use the testing utilities already in place.\n\nNow I'm thinking through task 2 for the ls utility—this is a new package, so I need to create both the main implementation and test files. The required reading should cover the SRD specification, how the architecture works, the system utilities package for file metadata, the formatting package for output, and the testing patterns used elsewhere. A key design decision is to install the SIGPIPE handler at startup, which is a pattern I see in other command implementations.\n\nFor the actual implementation, I'll lean on the sys package's Stat and Lstat functions to gather file information, use the format package to handle output presentation, and apply the differential testing approach against the standard ls binary to verify correctness. I'm also considering whether tasks 2 and 3 are realistic given the complexity of ls—the SRD has 53 total requirements, and my tasks cover the first 8 sub-requirements of R1, which likely handle the most fundamental operations like basic directory listing, single-column output, and long format display.\n\nA 300-500 line implementation for the initial ls package seems reasonable for covering those core features, with another similar chunk for the follow-up task. But I should verify whether I'm even starting with the right release—the constraint asks to focus on the earliest incomplete one, and I need to confirm that Release 03.1 is actually the pri